window.BioChecks[29] = {"2004-Q12a":[{"q":"What is homeostasis, and what is the role of the kidneys in it? (3 points)","right":"Keeping internal environment constant; kidneys balance water and salt","wrong":["Keeping internal environment constant; kidneys make urea from protein","Keeping internal environment constant; kidneys release insulin","Removing undigested food; kidneys balance water and salt"],"why":"Three points: homeostasis is keeping a constant internal environment; the kidneys keep the body's water balance and salt balance steady.","hint":"Homeo means 'same'. For the kidneys: what two things in urine change after lots of drink or crisps?","board":true}],"2004-Q12b":[{"q":"On a nephron diagram, which vessel carries blood into the glomerulus?","right":"Afferent arteriole","wrong":["Efferent arteriole","Renal venule","Peritubular capillary"],"why":"The afferent arteriole brings blood in (a for arrive); the efferent arteriole takes it out (e for exit).","hint":"Show the cup, the twisty tube with its hairpin, the duct, and the blood vessels in, out and around.","board":"Which blood vessel carries blood into the glomerulus of a nephron?"},{"q":"How do filtration and reabsorption occur in the nephron? (6 points)","right":"Pressure: plasma into capsule; osmosis from tubule to blood","wrong":["Pressure: proteins into capsule; osmosis from tubule to blood","Pressure: plasma into ureter; osmosis from blood to tubule","Low pressure: cells into capsule; osmosis from tubule to urine"],"why":"Three points each: high pressure forces plasma (not cells or proteins) into Bowman's capsule; the tubule returns water to the blood by osmosis.","hint":"Filtration: what pushes, and what's too big? Reabsorption: from where to where, and by what methods?","board":true}],"2005-Q1f":[{"q":"Getting rid of the waste made by metabolism is called what?","right":"Excretion","wrong":["Egestion","Secretion","Deamination"],"why":"Excretion removes waste cells make, like urea and CO2; egestion removes undigested food that cells never made.","hint":"Getting rid of faeces is egestion. What's the word for removing waste your cells made themselves?","board":true}],"2005-Q3a":[{"q":"True or false: urea is made in the kidneys.","right":"False","wrong":["True"],"why":"Urea is made in the liver by deamination; the kidneys only filter it out of the blood.","hint":"Urea comes from breaking down extra amino acids. Which organ does that job?","board":true}],"2006-Q6b":[{"q":"Distinguish between the ureter and the urethra. (2 points)","right":"Ureter: kidney to bladder; urethra: bladder to outside","wrong":["Ureter: bladder to outside; urethra: kidney to bladder","Ureter: kidney to bladder; urethra: bladder to kidney","Ureter: blood to kidney; urethra: bladder to outside"],"why":"Two points: the ureter carries urine from the kidney to the bladder; the urethra carries it from the bladder out of the body.","hint":"There are two of one tube but only one of the other. Where does each one start and end?","board":true}],"2006-Q13b":[{"q":"Explain the terms plasma and glomerular filtrate. (2 points)","right":"Plasma: liquid part of blood; filtrate: plasma minus its proteins","wrong":["Plasma: liquid part of blood; filtrate: plasma with all its proteins","Plasma: red cells of the blood; filtrate: plasma minus its proteins","Plasma: liquid part of blood; filtrate: urine stored in the bladder"],"why":"Two points: plasma is the liquid part of blood; glomerular filtrate is plasma, minus its proteins, that has passed into Bowman's capsule.","hint":"Plasma: what is blood without its cells? Filtrate: what gets into the capsule, and what can't?","board":true},{"q":"Why are red blood cells missing from glomerular filtrate?","right":"They are too big to pass into the capsule","wrong":["They are reabsorbed in the proximal tubule","They are broken down in the glomerulus","ADH stops them leaving the blood vessels"],"why":"Red cells are far too big to squeeze through the glomerulus wall into Bowman's capsule, so they stay in the blood.","hint":"Compare the size of a red blood cell with the tiny gaps in the glomerulus and capsule walls.","board":true},{"q":"Why does filtrate have the same glucose level as plasma?","right":"Glucose is small and passes freely through","wrong":["Glucose is pumped in by active transport","Insulin pushes glucose into the capsule","Glucose is made by cells of the capsule"],"why":"Glucose molecules are small enough to be filtered freely, so filtrate gets the same glucose level as plasma.","hint":"Think about the size of a glucose molecule next to the filter's pores, then compare with proteins.","board":"Why does glomerular filtrate have the same glucose level as blood plasma?"},{"q":"Why is there normally no glucose in urine?","right":"It is all reabsorbed into the blood","wrong":["It is too big to pass into the capsule","It is turned into urea in the kidney","ADH stops it from being filtered"],"why":"Glucose is filtered, but the proximal tubule reabsorbs all of it back into the blood, so none reaches the urine.","hint":"Glucose is filtered out, yet urine has none. Where does it go as filtrate flows along the tubule?","board":true},{"q":"After heavy exercise, why is a small volume of concentrated urine made? (one of 4 points)","right":"More ADH, so more water is reabsorbed","wrong":["More ADH, so less water is reabsorbed","Less ADH, so less water is reabsorbed","More insulin, so more water is reabsorbed"],"why":"Four of: sweating dehydrates you; the brain detects it; the pituitary secretes ADH; more water is reabsorbed in the collecting duct.","hint":"Start with how the athlete lost water, then follow the hormone message from the brain to the kidney.","board":true}],"2007-Q15c":[{"q":"What is homeostasis, and why is it important in the body? (3 points)","right":"Maintaining a constant internal environment, so enzymes work well","wrong":["Maintaining a constant internal environment, so no excretion is needed","Letting conditions inside match the outside, so enzymes work well","Removing undigested food from the gut, so enzymes work well"],"why":"Three points: homeostasis is the maintenance of a constant internal environment, which keeps conditions right for enzymes to work.","hint":"Homeo means 'same'. For why: which proteins run your cell reactions and need the right heat and pH?","board":true}],"2008-Q13a":[{"q":"Which best describes excretion?","right":"Removing waste made by the body's metabolism","wrong":["Removing undigested food from the gut","Releasing useful substances such as hormones","Breaking down large food molecules"],"why":"Excretion gets rid of waste made by cells, like urea and CO2; removing undigested food is egestion.","hint":"Compare it with egestion, which removes undigested food. Where does this other waste come from?","board":true},{"q":"Name a substance urea is made from, and one carbon dioxide is made from. (2 points)","right":"Urea from amino acids; carbon dioxide from glucose","wrong":["Urea from fatty acids; carbon dioxide from glucose","Urea from amino acids; carbon dioxide from water","Urea from glycerol; carbon dioxide from water"],"why":"Two points: urea comes from amino acids (protein) broken down in the liver; CO2 comes from respiring glucose (carbohydrate) or fat.","hint":"Urea contains nitrogen: which food has it? CO2 comes from breaking down a fuel in respiration.","board":true}],"2008-Q13b":[{"q":"On the diagram, which part is C?","right":"Proximal convoluted tubule","wrong":["Distal convoluted tubule","Collecting duct","Loop of Henle"],"why":"C comes straight after Bowman's capsule, so it is the proximal (first) tubule; the distal one comes after the loop.","hint":"A brings blood in. Then follow the filtrate: cup, first twisty tube, hairpin, second twist, duct.","board":false},{"q":"Arteriole A branches from which blood vessel?","right":"Renal artery","wrong":["Renal vein","Efferent arteriole","Inferior vena cava"],"why":"The renal artery brings blood into the kidney and branches into the arterioles that feed each glomerulus.","hint":"A brings blood to the filter. Which big vessel carries blood into the whole kidney?","board":"The arteriole that brings blood into a glomerulus branches from which blood vessel?"},{"q":"In which region of the kidney is Bowman's capsule (B)?","right":"Cortex","wrong":["Medulla","Pelvis","Pyramid"],"why":"Bowman's capsules and glomeruli sit in the outer cortex; the loops of Henle dip down into the medulla.","hint":"The kidney has an outer layer, striped pyramids and a funnel. Which one holds the filtering cups?","board":"In which region of the kidney is Bowman's capsule found?"},{"q":"In which part of the nephron are amino acids reabsorbed?","right":"Proximal convoluted tubule","wrong":["Distal convoluted tubule","Bowman's capsule","Collecting duct"],"why":"The proximal tubule reabsorbs all the glucose and amino acids; later parts mainly handle water and salts.","hint":"Filtration is where blood meets the nephron. Useful food molecules are taken back soon after.","board":true},{"q":"Give two features of the nephron that aid filtration. (2 points)","right":"Efferent arteriole narrower than afferent; walls one cell thick","wrong":["Afferent arteriole narrower than efferent; walls one cell thick","Efferent arteriole narrower than afferent; thick muscular walls","Small surface area; few, wide capillaries in the glomerulus"],"why":"Two of: efferent arteriole narrower than afferent (raises pressure), capillary walls one cell thick or porous, a large surface area.","hint":"Think of what raises the pressure in the knot of capillaries, and how thin and large the filter is.","board":true},{"q":"Which group of biomolecules is too big to be filtered?","right":"Proteins","wrong":["Glucose","Amino acids","Urea"],"why":"Proteins are big molecules that can't pass through the glomerulus wall; glucose and amino acids are small.","hint":"Think of the big molecules in plasma, such as antibodies, clotting factors and albumin.","board":"Which group of biomolecules in blood is too big to pass through the nephron's filter?"}],"2008-Q13c":[{"q":"Suggest two situations that may cause a drop in the water content of the blood. (2 points)","right":"Heavy sweating in hot weather, and not drinking enough water","wrong":["Heavy sweating in hot weather, and drinking lots of extra water","More ADH from the pituitary, and not drinking enough water","Drinking lots of extra water, and releasing more ADH"],"why":"Two of: sweating in heat or exercise, low water intake, high salt intake, an infection, or taking diuretics.","hint":"Think of one way you lose more water than usual, and one linked to what you eat or drink.","board":true},{"q":"Name the hormone released when blood water drops, and the gland that secretes it. (2 points)","right":"ADH, from the pituitary gland","wrong":["ADH, from the adrenal gland","Insulin, from the pituitary gland","Adrenaline, from the thyroid gland"],"why":"Two points: the hormone is ADH (anti-diuretic hormone), and it is secreted by the pituitary gland at the base of the brain.","hint":"The hormone's name means 'against making urine'. Its gland is pea-sized and hangs under the brain.","board":true},{"q":"Give a precise target area for ADH, and say how it reaches it. (2 points)","right":"The collecting duct; it travels there in the blood","wrong":["Bowman's capsule; it travels there in the blood","The collecting duct; it travels there along a nerve","The glomerulus; it travels there down the ureter"],"why":"Two points: ADH acts on the collecting duct (or distal tubule), and it reaches it in the bloodstream, like all hormones.","hint":"Think of the last tubes filtrate passes through before the pelvis. How do all hormones travel?","board":true},{"q":"When blood water is low, what does ADH do at its target?","right":"Walls more permeable, so more water is reabsorbed","wrong":["Walls less permeable, so more water stays in urine","Walls more permeable, so more glucose is reabsorbed","Walls less permeable, so less water is reabsorbed"],"why":"ADH makes the duct walls more permeable to water, so more water moves back into the blood. The scheme wants both halves.","hint":"Think what ADH changes about the duct walls, and which way water then moves by osmosis.","board":true}],"2009-Q15c":[{"q":"Write a short note on homeostasis. (3 points)","right":"Maintaining a constant internal environment, e.g. sweating to cool","wrong":["Maintaining a constant internal environment, e.g. shivering to cool","Letting conditions inside match the outside, e.g. sweating to cool","Removing undigested food from the gut, e.g. egesting faeces"],"why":"Three points: maintenance of a constant internal environment, plus an example of how, like sweating to lose heat when hot.","hint":"Homeo means 'same'. Add one thing the body keeps steady, how it does it, and why cells need that.","board":true}],"2010-Q15c":[{"q":"Which chain explains the athlete's concentrated urine?","right":"Sweat loss, more ADH, more water reabsorbed","wrong":["Sweat loss, less ADH, more water reabsorbed","Sweat loss, more ADH, less water reabsorbed","Extra urea, less ADH, less water reabsorbed"],"why":"Losing water in sweat leaves less water in the blood, so more ADH is released and the ducts reabsorb more water.","hint":"Where did the athlete's water go during exercise? Then which hormone tells the kidneys to save it?","board":"After heavy exercise, an athlete's urine is concentrated and low in volume. Which chain explains this?"}],"2011-Q12a":[{"q":"What does excretion mean?","right":"Getting rid of waste made by body cells","wrong":["Getting rid of food that was never digested","Releasing enzymes and hormones from glands","Breaking food down into small molecules"],"why":"Excretion removes wastes your own cells make, like urea; getting rid of undigested food is egestion.","hint":"Contrast it with egestion of undigested food. Where do waste products like urea and CO2 come from?","board":true}],"2011-Q12b":[{"q":"On a kidney section, which region collects urine before the ureter?","right":"Pelvis","wrong":["Cortex","Medulla","Pyramid"],"why":"The pelvis is the hollow space in the middle where urine collects before draining into the ureter.","hint":"Draw a bean: a dark outer band, striped pyramids inside, and a funnel space leading to the ureter.","board":"Which region of the kidney collects urine before it enters the ureter?"},{"q":"On a kidney section, where does reabsorption happen?","right":"In the tubules of the cortex and medulla","wrong":["In the pelvis, where urine collects","In the ureter, leaving the kidney","In the renal vein, leaving the kidney"],"why":"Reabsorption happens in the nephron tubules, found in the cortex and medulla; the pelvis and ureter only carry urine.","hint":"Where do the nephron's twisty tubes and loops lie in your drawing? Point there, not the funnel.","board":"Where in the kidney does reabsorption happen?"},{"q":"The renal artery branches off which blood vessel?","right":"Aorta","wrong":["Vena cava","Pulmonary artery","Hepatic artery"],"why":"The renal arteries branch straight off the aorta, the main artery from the heart; the vena cava is a vein.","hint":"1: it's named from the Latin word for kidney. 2: it branches off the body's biggest artery.","board":true},{"q":"In which body cavity are the kidneys?","right":"Abdominal cavity","wrong":["Thoracic cavity","Pelvic cavity","Pleural cavity"],"why":"The kidneys sit at the back of the abdomen, below the diaphragm; the thoracic cavity holds the heart and lungs.","hint":"Below the diaphragm, near the back: the same body cavity as the stomach and intestines.","board":true},{"q":"Which substance, other than water, is excreted in urine?","right":"Urea","wrong":["Glucose","Protein","Amino acids"],"why":"Urea, made from excess amino acids in the liver, leaves in urine; glucose and amino acids are normally reabsorbed.","hint":"Think of the nitrogen waste the liver makes from extra amino acids, or a mineral found in crisps.","board":true},{"q":"What shows the kidney works like an exocrine gland?","right":"It releases its product through a duct","wrong":["It releases hormones into the blood","It has a very rich blood supply","It filters blood under high pressure"],"why":"Exocrine glands release their products through ducts; urine leaves the kidney through a duct, the ureter.","hint":"Compare exocrine and endocrine glands: how does each send its product away from the gland?","board":true}],"2011-Q12c":[{"q":"On the diagram, what is part 4? (one of 8 points)","right":"Efferent arteriole","wrong":["Afferent arteriole","Renal venule","Renal artery"],"why":"Part 4 takes blood out (e for exit). 8 points: parts 1-6 (capsule, glomerulus, afferent, efferent, PCT, DCT), filtration at 1 or 2, and ADH.","hint":"Vessels: 'a' arrives, 'e' exits. Filtration: where is blood squeezed? 7: the water-saving hormone.","board":false},{"q":"Is protein in urine normal, and why?","right":"No, proteins are too big to be filtered","wrong":["Yes, proteins are small enough to filter","Yes, proteins are a normal waste product","No, ADH stops proteins being filtered"],"why":"Protein molecules are too big to pass through the glomerulus wall, so protein in urine suggests kidney damage.","hint":"Is protein meant to be filtered at all? Compare protein size with the gaps in the glomerulus wall.","board":true},{"q":"Is glucose in urine normal, and why?","right":"No, all glucose should be reabsorbed","wrong":["No, glucose is too big to be filtered","Yes, extra glucose is a waste product","Yes, glucose is never reabsorbed"],"why":"Glucose is filtered, but the proximal tubule should reabsorb all of it; glucose in urine can be a sign of diabetes.","hint":"Glucose is filtered out, but the body doesn't want to lose it. What should the tubules do with it?","board":true}],"2012-Q15c":[{"q":"What is homeostasis?","right":"Keeping the internal environment constant","wrong":["Removing the waste products of metabolism","Keeping the outside environment constant","Producing hormones to control growth"],"why":"Homeostasis keeps conditions inside the body, like temperature, pH and water, steady; removing waste is excretion.","hint":"Homeo means 'same' and stasis means 'standing'. Apply that to conditions inside the body.","board":true},{"q":"How does the liver help homeostasis?","right":"Stores or releases glucose as needed","wrong":["Filters urea out of the blood into urine","Excretes carbon dioxide from the body","Secretes insulin to lower blood sugar"],"why":"The liver stores glucose as glycogen and releases it when needed; insulin comes from the pancreas, not the liver.","hint":"Liver: what happens to blood sugar after a meal? Lungs: a waste you breathe out. Nephrons: urine.","board":true}],"2016-Q12a":[{"q":"Distinguish between excretion and egestion. (2 points)","right":"Excretion removes metabolic waste; egestion removes undigested food","wrong":["Excretion removes undigested food; egestion removes metabolic waste","Excretion removes metabolic waste; egestion releases gut enzymes","Excretion releases hormones; egestion removes undigested food"],"why":"Two points: excretion removes waste made by metabolism (like urea); egestion removes undigested food that was never absorbed.","hint":"Think of urea and CO2 for one, and faeces for the other. Where does each waste come from?","board":true}],"2016-Q12b":[{"q":"Name two excretory products of mammals, other than water. (2 points)","right":"Carbon dioxide and urea","wrong":["Oxygen and urea","Carbon dioxide and fibre","Glucose and amino acids"],"why":"Two of: carbon dioxide (from respiration), urea (from extra amino acids) and salts. Fibre is egested, not excreted.","hint":"Think of a gas you breathe out and a nitrogen waste made from extra amino acids.","board":true},{"q":"Where in the body are carbon dioxide and urea produced? (2 points)","right":"CO2 in all body cells (respiration); urea in the liver","wrong":["CO2 only in the lungs (breathing); urea in the liver","CO2 in all body cells (respiration); urea in the kidneys","CO2 in the liver (deamination); urea in the bladder"],"why":"Two points: CO2 is made in all body cells by respiration; urea is made in the liver by deamination. Lungs and kidneys only remove them.","hint":"Which process in every cell releases the gas? Which organ breaks down extra amino acids?","board":true},{"q":"Describe the role of ADH in excretion. (3 points)","right":"Collecting ducts more permeable, more water reabsorbed, less urine","wrong":["Collecting ducts less permeable, less water reabsorbed, more urine","Collecting ducts more permeable, more salt reabsorbed, more urine","Glomerulus more permeable, more water filtered, more urine"],"why":"Three of: released when dehydrated, acts on collecting ducts, makes their walls more permeable, more water reabsorbed, less urine made.","hint":"Start with what triggers ADH, then where it acts, what it does to the walls, and the urine.","board":true}],"2016-Q12c":[{"q":"What does the term homeostasis mean?","right":"Maintaining a steady internal environment","wrong":["Removing undigested food from the body","Letting inside conditions match outside","Breaking down excess amino acids"],"why":"Homeostasis keeps the inside of the body steady (temperature, pH, water) even when the outside changes.","hint":"Think of a thermostat keeping a room at one temperature. What does the body keep that way inside?","board":true}],"2016-Q15a":[{"q":"Which vein carries blood away from the kidneys?","right":"Renal vein","wrong":["Renal artery","Hepatic vein","Hepatic portal vein"],"why":"The renal vein drains blood from each kidney to the vena cava; the renal artery brings blood in.","hint":"It shares its first name with the artery that brings blood into the kidney.","board":true}],"2017-Q14c":[{"q":"How is the glomerulus suited to filtering blood? (2 points)","right":"High blood pressure, and capillary walls only one cell thick","wrong":["High blood pressure, and thick muscular walls to resist it","Low blood pressure, and capillary walls only one cell thick","A small surface area, and thick walls that stop leaks"],"why":"Two of: high blood pressure, capillary walls one cell thick, a large surface area, and being a knot of capillaries.","hint":"Think of the push that forces fluid out of the blood, and how thin and big the filter surface is.","board":true}],"2017-Q15a":[{"q":"Which phrase explains homeostasis?","right":"Keeping conditions inside the body steady","wrong":["Keeping conditions outside the body steady","Getting rid of waste made by cells","Breaking food down to release energy"],"why":"Homeostasis is about the internal environment staying constant; getting rid of cell waste is excretion.","hint":"Your body stays near 37 degrees whatever the weather. Put that idea in general terms.","board":true},{"q":"Why does the body need homeostasis?","right":"So enzymes get the right temperature and pH","wrong":["So the body no longer needs to excrete","So blood glucose can rise freely after meals","So body temperature can match the weather"],"why":"Enzymes only work well in a narrow range of temperature and pH, so keeping these steady keeps metabolism going.","hint":"What molecules control every reaction in cells, and stop working at the wrong heat or pH?","board":true}],"2018-Q6":[{"q":"On the diagram, which structure is Y?","right":"Distal convoluted tubule","wrong":["Proximal convoluted tubule","Collecting duct","Loop of Henle"],"why":"Y comes after the loop of Henle, so it is the distal (far) tubule; the proximal one is right after Bowman's capsule.","hint":"X is the cup round the knot of capillaries. Y is the twisty tube after the loop, far from the cup.","board":false},{"q":"Where on the diagram does ADH act?","right":"Collecting duct","wrong":["Glomerulus","Afferent arteriole","Proximal convoluted tubule"],"why":"ADH targets the collecting duct (and distal tubule), the last parts of the nephron, where water can be saved.","hint":"ADH acts late, on the tubes that carry filtrate down towards the pelvis just before it is urine.","board":"Which part of the nephron does the hormone ADH act on?"},{"q":"What does ADH do to its target area?","right":"Makes it more permeable to water","wrong":["Makes it less permeable to water","Makes it pump glucose into urine","Makes it let protein into urine"],"why":"With ADH the duct walls let more water through, so more water is reabsorbed into the blood.","hint":"Does ADH help the body keep water or lose it? How must the tube walls change to do that?","board":true},{"q":"What change in the blood triggers ADH release?","right":"Higher salt, less water in the blood","wrong":["Lower salt, more water in the blood","Higher blood volume after drinking","Higher carbon dioxide in the blood"],"why":"When blood gets too salty (too little water), the brain detects it and ADH is released to save water.","hint":"Think about why you feel thirsty after a bag of crisps. What has changed in the blood's make-up?","board":true},{"q":"In which gland is ADH made?","right":"Hypothalamus","wrong":["Adrenal gland","Thyroid gland","Pancreas"],"why":"ADH is made in the hypothalamus and released from the pituitary; the scheme accepts either.","hint":"It's made in a part of the brain just above the pituitary, which only stores and releases it.","board":true},{"q":"Which is a treatment for kidney failure?","right":"Kidney dialysis","wrong":["Insulin injections","A course of antibiotics","Blood transfusion"],"why":"A dialysis machine filters urea and extra salt from the blood when the kidneys fail; a transplant also works.","hint":"Think of a machine that cleans the blood, or replacing the organ with one from a donor.","board":true}],"2020-Q13a":[{"q":"What is meant by homeostasis?","right":"Maintaining a stable internal environment","wrong":["Maintaining a stable external environment","Removing the waste of metabolism","Changing body temperature with the weather"],"why":"Homeostasis keeps the body's internal conditions steady; removing metabolic waste is excretion.","hint":"Think of how blood sugar, temperature and water levels are all kept within narrow limits.","board":true},{"q":"Why is homeostasis important in organisms?","right":"Lets enzymes and metabolism work properly","wrong":["Stops cells from needing any energy","Lets body pH change freely with diet","Lets cells take in water without limit"],"why":"Enzymes need a steady temperature and pH, so a constant internal environment keeps metabolism running well.","hint":"What would happen to the proteins that speed up cell reactions if you overheated?","board":true},{"q":"What is meant by excretion?","right":"Removal of metabolic waste from the body","wrong":["Removal of undigested food from the body","Keeping a constant internal environment","Release of energy from food in cells"],"why":"Excretion removes waste made by metabolism, like urea and CO2; removing undigested food is egestion.","hint":"Think about urea, CO2 and salts: where are they made, and what must the body do with them?","board":true}],"2020-Q13b":[{"q":"Where in the body is urea made?","right":"Liver","wrong":["Kidney","Bladder","Pancreas"],"why":"The liver breaks down excess amino acids (deamination) to make urea; the kidneys only remove it.","hint":"Which large organ breaks down extra amino acids, in a process called deamination?","board":true},{"q":"Which food biomolecule is urea made from?","right":"Protein","wrong":["Carbohydrate","Lipid","Vitamins"],"why":"Proteins contain nitrogen; extra amino acids are deaminated and the nitrogen ends up in urea. Carbs and fats have none.","hint":"Urea has nitrogen in it. Which main food biomolecule contains nitrogen, unlike fats and sugars?","board":true},{"q":"In which part of the kidney is the loop of Henle found?","right":"Medulla","wrong":["Cortex","Pelvis","Ureter"],"why":"The loops of Henle dip down into the medulla; Bowman's capsules and the convoluted tubules are in the cortex.","hint":"The cups sit in the outer layer, the loops dip into the pyramids, and A leads to the bladder.","board":true},{"q":"In order, which parts follow Bowman's capsule in a nephron?","right":"PCT, loop of Henle, DCT, collecting duct","wrong":["DCT, loop of Henle, PCT, collecting duct","Loop of Henle, PCT, DCT, collecting duct","PCT, DCT, loop of Henle, collecting duct"],"why":"Filtrate flows from the capsule to the proximal tubule (PCT), down the loop of Henle, to the distal tubule (DCT).","hint":"Show the cup and knot, two twisty tubes joined by a hairpin, a duct, and capillaries round them.","board":true}],"2020-Q13c":[{"q":"From the table, what is the main difference between plasma and filtrate, and why? (2 points)","right":"Plasma has protein, filtrate has none; protein is too big to pass","wrong":["Filtrate has protein, plasma has none; protein is made in the capsule","Plasma has protein, filtrate has none; ADH stops protein passing","Plasma has glucose, filtrate has none; glucose is too big to pass"],"why":"Two points: plasma has protein (7 g/100 cm3) but filtrate has none, because protein molecules are too big to pass out of the glomerulus.","hint":"Find the substance with a value in plasma but zero in the filtrate. Think about molecule size.","board":"What is the main difference between blood plasma and glomerular filtrate, and why? (2 points)"},{"q":"From the table, why is there glucose in filtrate but none in urine?","right":"All the glucose is reabsorbed into blood","wrong":["Glucose is too big to enter the filtrate","Glucose is turned into urea in the tubule","Glucose is used up by the bladder wall"],"why":"Glucose is filtered (0.1 in both plasma and filtrate), then all of it is reabsorbed in the tubule, so urine has 0.","hint":"Glucose: same in plasma and filtrate, then zero in urine. Think molecule size, then the tubules.","board":"Why is there glucose in glomerular filtrate but normally none in urine?"},{"q":"What does a high salt intake do to urine volume, and why?","right":"Less urine, as more ADH is released","wrong":["More urine, as more ADH is released","More urine, as less ADH is released","Less urine, as less ADH is released"],"why":"Salty blood triggers more ADH, so the collecting ducts reabsorb more water and less urine is made.","hint":"Salty blood means too little water. Which hormone is released, and how does it change water loss?","board":true}],"2021-Q15a":[{"q":"What does homeostasis mean?","right":"Keeping a stable internal environment","wrong":["Keeping the external environment stable","Removing toxic waste made by cells","Controlling growth and development"],"why":"Homeostasis means the body keeps its inside conditions, like temperature and water level, stable.","hint":"Homeo means 'similar'. Think of conditions like temperature and water level inside your body.","board":true}],"2022-Q15a":[{"q":"What do the terms excretion and homeostasis mean? (2 points)","right":"Removing metabolic waste; keeping a constant internal environment","wrong":["Removing undigested food; keeping a constant internal environment","Removing metabolic waste; letting conditions match the outside","Releasing hormones into blood; removing undigested food"],"why":"Two points: excretion is removing waste made by metabolism; homeostasis is keeping a constant internal environment.","hint":"Excretion: waste made by what? Homeostasis: 'homeo' means 'same', so what stays the same?","board":true}],"2022-Q15b":[{"q":"What does the fat layer around the kidneys do?","right":"Protects them from knocks and bumps","wrong":["Stores urine before it goes to the bladder","Makes ADH to control water balance","Filters blood before it enters"],"why":"The fat cushions the kidneys against knocks (and insulates them); urine is stored in the bladder, not the fat.","hint":"Think of why bubble wrap goes round fragile things in a parcel.","board":true},{"q":"On the kidney diagram, which region is B?","right":"Medulla","wrong":["Cortex","Pelvis","Ureter"],"why":"B is the middle layer with the pyramids: the medulla. The cortex (A) is outside it and the pelvis (C) inside.","hint":"Outer layer, then the striped pyramids in the middle, then the funnel leading to the ureter.","board":false},{"q":"In which kidney region does filtration happen?","right":"Cortex (A)","wrong":["Medulla (B)","Pelvis (C)","Ureter"],"why":"Filtration happens at the glomerulus and Bowman's capsule, which are found in the outer cortex.","hint":"Where in the kidney do the glomeruli and their cups sit: outer layer, pyramids or funnel?","board":false},{"q":"Why does blood enter the glomerulus under high pressure?","right":"To force small molecules out into the capsule","wrong":["To force proteins out into the capsule","To push filtrate back into the blood","To stop any plasma leaving the blood"],"why":"High pressure squeezes water and small molecules out of the blood into Bowman's capsule: that is filtration.","hint":"Think of pushing water through a coffee filter. What would happen with no push at all?","board":true},{"q":"Why are the PCT, loop of Henle and DCT surrounded by so many capillaries? (2 points)","right":"Reabsorption into the blood, and secretion of wastes into tubules","wrong":["Reabsorption into the blood, and making ADH for the tubules","Pushing proteins into tubules, and secretion of wastes into them","Carrying urine to the bladder, and making urea from amino acids"],"why":"Two points: reabsorption (water, glucose and salts go back into the blood) and secretion (extra wastes pass from the blood into the tubules).","hint":"Things move both ways between tubule and blood. Name the process for each direction.","board":true},{"q":"How does more ADH change urine volume?","right":"Lowers it: more water is reabsorbed","wrong":["Raises it: less water is reabsorbed","Lowers it: less water is filtered","Raises it: more salt is excreted"],"why":"ADH makes the collecting duct more permeable, so more water returns to the blood and a small volume of urine forms.","hint":"'Diuresis' means making lots of urine, and ADH is anti-diuretic. So what does 'anti' do?","board":true},{"q":"Urine leaves C and travels to the bladder through which tube?","right":"Ureter","wrong":["Urethra","Collecting duct","Renal vein"],"why":"The ureter carries urine from the kidney's pelvis to the bladder; the urethra takes it from the bladder out.","hint":"There are two of these tubes, one from each kidney's funnel down to the bladder.","board":"Urine leaves the renal pelvis and travels to the bladder through which tube?"}],"2023-Q6d":[{"q":"Why is urine volume low if you don't drink much?","right":"Collecting ducts reabsorb more water","wrong":["Collecting ducts reabsorb less water","The pituitary releases less ADH","Bowman's capsule filters more water"],"why":"Low water in the blood triggers more ADH, so the collecting ducts reabsorb more water and less urine is made.","hint":"With less water in the blood, which hormone rises, and what does it make the kidneys do with water?","board":true}],"2023-Q17c":[{"q":"Which vein carries blood with the least metabolic waste?","right":"Renal vein","wrong":["Hepatic vein","Hepatic portal vein","Inferior vena cava"],"why":"Blood leaving the kidneys has just had urea and other wastes filtered out, so the renal vein has the least.","hint":"Which organ cleans waste such as urea out of the blood? Name the vein leaving it.","board":true}],"2024-Q17c":[{"q":"What is the name for keeping a constant internal environment?","right":"Homeostasis","wrong":["Excretion","Osmoregulation","Metabolism"],"why":"Homeostasis is the general term; osmoregulation is just the water and salt part of it.","hint":"It comes from Greek words for 'same' and 'standing still'.","board":true},{"q":"On a nephron diagram, which structure cups the glomerulus?","right":"Bowman's capsule","wrong":["Loop of Henle","Collecting duct","Distal tubule"],"why":"Label it with the others: Bowman's capsule (the cup round the glomerulus), glomerulus, PCT, loop of Henle, DCT, collecting duct.","hint":"Show the cup, the twists and loop, the duct, and the blood vessels in, out and around the tubes.","board":"In a nephron, which structure cups the glomerulus?"},{"q":"Which is a way blood differs from glomerular filtrate?","right":"Blood has cells; filtrate has none","wrong":["Filtrate has cells; blood has none","Blood has glucose; filtrate has none","Blood has urea; filtrate has none"],"why":"Blood cells (and plasma proteins) are too big to be filtered; glucose and urea pass into the filtrate.","hint":"What parts of blood are too big to be squeezed through the glomerulus wall?","board":true},{"q":"Which part of the nephron reabsorbs water?","right":"Loop of Henle","wrong":["Glomerulus","Bowman's capsule","Renal pelvis"],"why":"Water is reabsorbed along the tubules (PCT, loop, DCT, collecting duct); the glomerulus is where filtering happens.","hint":"1: name tubes the filtrate passes after the cup. 2: think of useful food molecules and minerals.","board":true}],"2025-Q17c":[{"q":"What is homeostasis?","right":"Maintaining a constant internal environment","wrong":["Maintaining a constant rate of growth","Removing metabolic waste from the body","Maintaining a constant external environment"],"why":"Homeostasis keeps internal conditions like temperature, pH and water constant; removing waste is excretion.","hint":"Think about what stays the same inside you even when it's freezing or boiling outside.","board":true},{"q":"Which endocrine gland secretes ADH?","right":"Pituitary gland","wrong":["Adrenal gland","Thyroid gland","Pineal gland"],"why":"ADH is released from the pituitary at the base of the brain; the adrenal glands make adrenaline.","hint":"The 'master gland': pea-sized and hanging just under the brain.","board":true},{"q":"Which part of the nephron does ADH affect?","right":"Distal convoluted tubule","wrong":["Proximal convoluted tubule","Bowman's capsule","Glomerulus"],"why":"ADH acts on the distal tubule and collecting duct, making their walls let more water back into the blood.","hint":"ADH acts near the end of the nephron, after the loop. Name one part there.","board":true},{"q":"What does more ADH do to the volume of urine?","right":"Less urine is produced","wrong":["More urine is produced","The volume stays the same","More dilute urine is produced"],"why":"More ADH means more water is reabsorbed, so a smaller volume of more concentrated urine is made.","hint":"ADH helps the body hold on to water. So what happens to the amount of urine?","board":true},{"q":"From the graphs, how do blood volume and ADH change during exercise?","right":"Blood volume falls as ADH rises","wrong":["Blood volume rises as ADH rises","Blood volume falls as ADH falls","Blood volume falls, ADH stays level"],"why":"Sweating cuts blood volume, so more ADH is released to save water: volume goes down while ADH goes up.","hint":"Compare the trends of X and Y. Then how do you replace lost water, and where would ADH settle?","board":"During vigorous exercise, how do an athlete's blood volume and ADH level change?"},{"q":"How can the skin help the athlete's homeostasis during exercise? (2 points)","right":"Sweating, and widening of skin blood vessels (vasodilation)","wrong":["Shivering, and absorbing water back in through the skin","Releasing ADH, and making extra urea in the skin cells","Releasing insulin, and storing extra glucose as glycogen"],"why":"Two points: sweating (evaporation carries heat away) and vasodilation (wider skin vessels lose more heat). Shivering is done by muscles.","hint":"Think of what forms on a hot athlete's skin, and how its blood vessels change to lose heat.","board":"How can the skin help an athlete's homeostasis during vigorous exercise? (2 points)"}],"2026-Q17b":[{"q":"What is the ability to keep a constant internal environment called?","right":"Homeostasis","wrong":["Excretion","Thermoregulation","Metabolism"],"why":"Homeostasis covers all internal conditions; thermoregulation only covers temperature.","hint":"Think of the word that links sweating, shivering and ADH: they all keep the inside steady.","board":true},{"q":"Why must organisms keep a constant internal environment?","right":"So enzymes can work efficiently","wrong":["So body temperature can match the air","So no waste is ever produced","So cells can take in water freely"],"why":"Enzymes need the right temperature and pH; keeping these constant lets metabolism run efficiently.","hint":"Think of what happens to the catalysts in your cells if the body gets too hot or too acidic.","board":true}],"2026-Q17c":[{"q":"What does excretion mean?","right":"Removing the waste products of metabolism","wrong":["Removing fibre and undigested food","Releasing enzymes from a gland","Taking useful substances back into blood"],"why":"Excretion removes waste made by the body's chemical reactions, like urea; removing fibre is egestion.","hint":"Waste from cell reactions, like urea and CO2, not undigested food. What does the body do with it?","board":true},{"q":"Which way should the urine arrow on the ureter point?","right":"From kidney to bladder","wrong":["From bladder to kidney","From bladder to urethra","From kidney to urethra"],"why":"Urine is made in the kidney and flows down the ureter into the bladder; the urethra then carries it out.","hint":"Draw the organ pair, a tube from each to the storage bag, and the exit tube. Where is urine made?","board":"In which direction does urine flow through the ureter?"},{"q":"In part B (loop of Henle), what happens?","right":"Water and salts are reabsorbed","wrong":["Blood is filtered under pressure","ADH is made and sent to the blood","Glucose is moved into the urine"],"why":"The loop of Henle reabsorbs water and salts; filtering under pressure happens at A, Bowman's capsule.","hint":"A is the cup round the capillary knot; B is the hairpin in the medulla. Think filter vs take back.","board":"What happens in the loop of Henle during urine production?"},{"q":"Why is there normally no protein in urine?","right":"Protein is too big to be filtered","wrong":["ADH stops protein being filtered","Protein is turned into urea first","Protein is used up by kidney cells"],"why":"Protein molecules are too big to pass through the glomerulus wall into Bowman's capsule, so they stay in the blood.","hint":"Think of the glomerulus as a sieve. How do protein molecules compare to its holes?","board":true}],"S1-Q1e":[{"q":"On the diagram, which part is A?","right":"Proximal convoluted tubule","wrong":["Distal convoluted tubule","Collecting duct","Bowman's capsule"],"why":"A comes straight after Bowman's capsule, before the loop of Henle, so it is the proximal (first) tubule.","hint":"A is the first twisty tube after the cup; B is the hairpin that dips into the medulla.","board":false},{"q":"Where on the diagram should you write F for ultrafiltration?","right":"On the glomerulus","wrong":["On the loop of Henle","On the collecting duct","On the distal tubule"],"why":"Ultrafiltration happens where blood under pressure in the glomerulus is squeezed into Bowman's capsule.","hint":"Ultrafiltration happens where the blood vessel forms a tight knot inside a cup.","board":"On a nephron diagram, where would you mark the site of ultrafiltration?"},{"q":"Which part of the blood is not normally found in urine?","right":"Plasma proteins","wrong":["Urea","Water","Mineral salts"],"why":"Plasma proteins are too big to be filtered; urea, water and salts all end up in urine.","hint":"Which large molecules in plasma, like antibodies, are too big to pass the filter?","board":true},{"q":"How does a longer loop of Henle help a desert animal?","right":"More water is reabsorbed into the blood","wrong":["More water is lost in dilute urine","Blood is filtered at a higher pressure","More glucose is reabsorbed into the blood"],"why":"A longer loop lets more water be reabsorbed, so the animal makes concentrated urine and saves water.","hint":"Camels rarely get to drink. What job does the loop do, and what would a longer one do more of?","board":true}]};
